# Declares that this repo deploys as part of /sprint-ship (Phase 7).
# One step per line, in order, run from the merged `main` after the squash
# merge and local cleanup. `recipe: <name>` runs `just <name>`; a bare line
# would name a skill.
#
# Filed as WI 2798 during sprint 013's ship: kdashdata publishes `kdash-pub` to
# the package store and installs it on four hosts, but declared nothing — so
# Phase 7 was skipped silently on every ship, and sprints 010, 011 and 013 all
# deployed because a human or an overseer asked in that session, never because
# the repo said it deploys.
#
# `publish` is safe to declare unconditionally because sprint 015 made it
# SELF-SKIPPING: it derives its version from the binary's own inputs
# (`just version` — `publishers/rust/src`, `build.rs`, `Cargo.toml`,
# `Cargo.lock`; NOT `HEAD`) and exits 0 with "nothing to publish" when the
# store already holds it. That matters more here than in most repos, because
# most kdashdata sprints are contract-only — sprint 012 was schema and docs
# with no publisher change, and an unconditional publish would have cut a new
# version and reinstalled it on four hosts for nothing.
#
# `deploy-all` runs UNCONDITIONALLY, including when publish skipped. A host can
# be behind the store's `latest` without this sprint changing anything —
# komarchy was ten days behind in sprint 011 — so "publish no-op'd, therefore
# skip the deploy" is not right. It installs `latest`, which is unchanged in
# that case, and knarr's confirm step still reports what each host is running.
#
# `deploy-all` rather than `deploy`: all four publisher hosts, because the
# failure this exists to prevent is deploying MOST of them. komarchy is asleep
# most of the time and its skip is a NORMAL outcome — the recipe probes it from
# this host, prints the skip, and exits 0.
recipe: publish
recipe: deploy-all
